  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "atom-1",
      "text": "Even if your mail-in ballot is postmarked prior to the Election day but arrives after the Election day it will be thrown out.",
      "sourceText": "Even if your mail-in ballot is postmarked prior to the Election day but arrives after the Election day it will be thrown out.",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 125,
      "role": "CAUSAL_RELATION"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-836f4baee804::sentence-10",
          "documentId": "doc-836f4baee804",
          "text": "Most states require that your ballot be postmarked on Election Day, Nov. 3, but even if they’re postmarked in time, some will not accept those ballots if they arrive too late; the specific rules vary state by state. ",
          "start": 771,
          "end": 987,
          "context": "Many states suggest sending your ballot at least one week before Election Day (that’s today) to make sure it arrives in time.\n Most states require that your ballot be postmarked on Election Day, Nov. 3, but even if they’re postmarked in time, some will not accept those ballots if they arrive too late; the specific rules vary state by state.  Here is the latest possible date in each state when absentee ballots will be accepted; these may not apply to voters overseas or in the military who are subject to special deadlines.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-836f4baee804::sentence-18",
          "documentId": "doc-836f4baee804",
          "text": "One state, Ohio, requires that ballots be postmarked by Nov. 2, but they can arrive as late as the close of business on Nov. 13, 10 full days after Election Day, and still be counted.\n",
          "start": 1587,
          "end": 1771,
          "context": "MST.\n One state, Ohio, requires that ballots be postmarked by Nov. 2, but they can arrive as late as the close of business on Nov. 13, 10 full days after Election Day, and still be counted.\n Most states require mail-in ballots to be received by the time the polls close in the state on Election Day, usually 7 p.m. or 8 p.m. local time, but some allow ballots received after Election Day to be counted. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-836f4baee804::sentence-9",
          "documentId": "doc-836f4baee804",
          "text": "Many states suggest sending your ballot at least one week before Election Day (that’s today) to make sure it arrives in time.\n",
          "start": 645,
          "end": 771,
          "context": "In order to ensure your ballot is counted, officials suggest mail-in ballots be sent in early, especially given USPS delays.  Many states suggest sending your ballot at least one week before Election Day (that’s today) to make sure it arrives in time.\n Most states require that your ballot be postmarked on Election Day, Nov. 3, but even if they’re postmarked in time, some will not accept those ballots if they arrive too late; the specific rules vary state by state. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-836f4baee804::sentence-19",
          "documentId": "doc-836f4baee804",
          "text": "Most states require mail-in ballots to be received by the time the polls close in the state on Election Day, usually 7 p.m. or 8 p.m. local time, but some allow ballots received after Election Day to be counted. ",
          "start": 1771,
          "end": 1983,
          "context": "One state, Ohio, requires that ballots be postmarked by Nov. 2, but they can arrive as late as the close of business on Nov. 13, 10 full days after Election Day, and still be counted.\n Most states require mail-in ballots to be received by the time the polls close in the state on Election Day, usually 7 p.m. or 8 p.m. local time, but some allow ballots received after Election Day to be counted.  Washington state, which votes entirely by mail, has the latest deadline, allowing ballots received through Nov. 23 to be counted.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-836f4baee804::sentence-21",
          "documentId": "doc-836f4baee804",
          "text": "For months leading up to Election Day, President Donald Trump has baselessly attacked mail-in voting, but the process is safe and secure: According to the Brennan Center for Justice, Oregon, another fully vote-by-mail state, has found fraud in just 0.00001% of all votes cast by mail since the year 2000.\n",
          "start": 2113,
          "end": 2418,
          "context": "Washington state, which votes entirely by mail, has the latest deadline, allowing ballots received through Nov. 23 to be counted.\n For months leading up to Election Day, President Donald Trump has baselessly attacked mail-in voting, but the process is safe and secure: According to the Brennan Center for Justice, Oregon, another fully vote-by-mail state, has found fraud in just 0.00001% of all votes cast by mail since the year 2000.\n There are pending legal fights over the absentee ballot deadlines in two battleground states — North Carolina and Pennsylvania. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-188db306f350::sentence-38",
          "documentId": "doc-188db306f350",
          "text": "\"If you wait until the deadline, you risk not getting your ballot in time to vote it and return it by 8 p.m. on Election Day.\"\n",
          "start": 5610,
          "end": 5737,
          "context": "\"Please do not wait for the legal deadline – which is 5 p.m. on Thursday, October 29 – for your clerk to receive your request for an absentee ballot by mail,\" Wolfe said in a statement.  \"If you wait until the deadline, you risk not getting your ballot in time to vote it and return it by 8 p.m. on Election Day.\"\n It can take up to seven days for a ballot to travel through the United States Postal Service, but voters can also hand deliver their completed ballots to a local clerk's office or drop box before Election Day."
        }
      ]
    }
